    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3165825e_51ef0d17",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1930620
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-25T23:26:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Adapt swap_cgroup_cmpxchg to no longer rely on the global swap cgroup lock,\n\nThat\u0027s unfortunate...\n\n`swap_cgroup_cmpxchg` is also only for v1\u0027s `move_charge_at_immigrate` which has been deprecated since 2022, and was removed a year ago.\n\n`move_charge_at_immigrate` and memcg v1 are dead ends. You need to move off of them.\n\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/all/20221206171340.139790-4-hannes@cmpxchg.org/\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/all/20241025012304.2473312-1-shakeel.butt@linux.dev/\n\n---\n\nHowever the rest of the patches in this series look like they are worth taking (especially `mm/swap_cgroup: remove global swap cgroup lock`), and `swap_cgroup_cmpxchg` blocks that.\n\nThis conflict *seems* small enough to be manageable but even what\u0027s implemented here looks like it should be retrying the `cmpxchg` if it fails, like `__swap_cgroup_id_xchg` does. I don\u0027t like the idea of maintaining out of tree cgroup v1 code, and especially not any charge moving code.",
      "parentUuid": "1860a968_9a14f0c4",
      "revId": "8e677e18c907dc9726ef4787f92e72066973ecda",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "370b2b4f_f05f5e2d",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 4361992
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-26T03:48:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The __swap_cgroup_id_xchg function’s return value semantics are that it performs the change (chg) and returns the old_id, so it uses a while loop to retry until the change succeeds. In contrast, the swap_cgroup_cmpxchg function’s return value semantics are: if the change succeeds, it returns the old_id; if the change fails, it returns 0. Therefore, there is no need to add a retry mechanism.\nAlternatively, we could add a limited number of retries to increase the success rate of swap_cgroup_cmpxchg, like this:\n\nint retry_cnt \u003d 5;\n\ndo {\n\told_ids \u003d atomic_read(\u0026sc-\u003eids);\n\tshift \u003d (offset % ID_PER_SC) * ID_SHIFT;\n\tnew_ids \u003d (old_ids \u0026 ~(ID_MASK \u003c\u003c shift));\n\tnew_ids |\u003d ((unsigned int)new) \u003c\u003c shift;\n\tret \u003d atomic_try_cmpxchg(\u0026sc-\u003eids, \u0026old_ids, new_ids);\n} while ((retry_cnt--) \u0026\u0026 (!ret));\n\n\nMaintaining these redundant codes is indeed not a good idea.\nWe can control this part of the code with CONFIG_MEMCG_V1 and remove it during the next KMI change window.",
      "parentUuid": "3165825e_51ef0d17",
      "revId": "8e677e18c907dc9726ef4787f92e72066973ecda",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e286bea_28fdb292",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
